  "authorshipStatement": "AI generated following prompts by Murdoch J. Gabbay",
  "documentationRevision": 2,
  "documentationRevisionDate": "2026-10-14",
  "narrative": {
    "cover": [
      [
        "Abstract",
        "This catalogue documents all 89 curated configurations in Aperiodic Life 3.7.4: 22 on the Einstein-hat tiling, 21 on Penrose rhombs, 36 on the hexagonal lattice and 10 on the square lattice. Every entry has its exact rule, initialisation, production-engine illustrations, computational evidence and attribution. All 56 complete rule-sets are registered, and all 12 named rule presets are described separately."
      ],
      [
        "Scope of originality",
        "The catalogue identifies 55 configurations original to this package for which no exact precedent was found in the literature reviewed to 14 October 2026. A further package search result, P09, may be Owens and Stepneyâ€™s published fireworks oscillator [5,6]; its identity remains unresolved. Four deliberate package constructions, 25 published examples and four adaptations complete the 89 entries. Each adaptation names its parent pattern or rule and states what changed."
      ],
      [
        "Authorship and status",
        "AI generated following prompts by Murdoch J. Gabbay. Catalogue revision 2, 14 October 2026, for Aperiodic Life 3.7.4. The software package was vibe-coded by Murdoch J. Gabbay. This catalogue reports computational checks and source comparisons; it is not a peer-reviewed paper. Each reproduced pattern and rule names its source with a numbered reference."
      ]
    ],
    "scope": [
      [
        "What is included",
        "Every entry in discoveries.js appears once, with a stable identifier and a linked rule reference. The 12 named options in the separate rule selector also appear. The Custom option, arbitrary painted states and arbitrary random seeds are not finite preset lists. Penrose sun/star selects geometry, not a cellular-automaton pattern. Spectre and Goucher API-only tables are distinguished from the UI presets."
      ],
      [
        "Original to this package, or already known?",
        "ORIGINAL TO THIS PACKAGE identifies a configuration generated by this packageâ€™s search, with no exact precedent identified in the literature reviewed to 14 October 2026. It does not attribute an established update rule or travelling mechanism to this package, and the review cannot exclude unexamined or unpublished earlier work. CONSTRUCTED HERE identifies a deliberate assembly and names its underlying rule or principle. PUBLISHED names the author, pattern or mechanism and numbered source. ADAPTATION names that predecessor, its source and the change. P09 is labelled POSSIBLE PUBLISHED MATCH, not original."
      ],
      [
        "What has been compared",
        "The audit uses Owens and Stepneyâ€™s Penrose catalogue [5,6], Goucherâ€™s Penrose-glider paper [4], Edmondâ€™s paper and compendium [2,3], the hexagonal seed records identified in X23-X35, Stephen Silverâ€™s Life Lexicon [24,25,26,27,29,30,32], and retained exact-state comparisons. Eleven hexagonal precedents have executable comparison records. Coverage is incomplete: the wider hexagonal databases and unpublished work have not been exhausted. A failed search or inaccessible web page is not evidence of originality."
      ],
      [
        "How to use this document",
        "Start with the plain-language rule guide, then the neighbourhood diagrams and rule-status definitions. Tiling directories link to every illustrated configuration. H, P, X and S label Hat, Penrose, hexagonal and square entries; C01-C04 retain the earlier catalogueâ€™s constructed-example labels. R01-R56 denote complete rule-sets. U01-U12 denote rule-selector templates, which need a chosen graph. These labels are document references, not standard names."
      ]
    ],
    "rules": [
      [
        "Update convention",
        "Each polygon is one cell. All cells update simultaneously using the states before the update. A complete rule specifies the tiling, neighbour relation, transition rule and total state count C. A B/S string alone does not specify all four. The cell itself is never counted as its neighbour."
      ],
      [
        "Worked example: R40",
        "R40 is B234/S245 with C = 5 on the shared-edge hexagonal graph. An empty cell is born with exactly 2, 3 or 4 live neighbours. A live cell survives with exactly 2, 4 or 5. The six neighbours are precisely the edge-sharing hexagons. Thus R40:S5 means survival with exactly five of those six neighbours live. S5 is one clause, not an entire rule. The example is X08, the ten-step knightship launcher."
      ],
      [
        "States and refractory time",
        "State 0 is empty and state 1 is live. Only state 1 contributes to B/S neighbour counts. With C = 2, a live cell that fails survival becomes 0. With C > 2, it becomes state 2; refractory states then advance one step each generation, regardless of neighbours, through 2, 3, ..., C-1 and back to 0. A refractory cell cannot be born until it is 0 again. In R40 the death sequence is 1, 2, 3, 4, 0."
      ],
      [
        "Exact counts, not thresholds",
        "B12 means one or two neighbours, not twelve. B2/S has no survival counts, whereas B2/S0 permits survival with zero live neighbours. A multi-digit count is bracketed, for example B[10]/S[9,10]. None of R01-R56 needs a multi-digit B/S count. By contrast, priority-table conditions n_i >= k mean at least k neighbours in state i, including i = 0."
      ],
      [
        "Priority tables are different",
        "Named TABLE rules count each neighbour state separately and take the first matching ordered row. All conditions in that row must hold. No match gives state 0. The higher states are signals, with no automatic refractory ageing. The complete ordered rows, including defaults and state totals, appear in the rule register."
      ]
    ],
    "evidence": [
      [
        "Exact cycles and regular-lattice spaceships",
        "A stationary cycle satisfies F^p(S) = S on the same graph; its least positive p is its period. A transient of a steps means that the first recurrent phase is F^a(S). Equality concerns the entire labelled state. On an infinite translation-invariant lattice, F^p(S) = T_d(S) with nonzero displacement d and a verified empty exterior implies F^(kp)(S) = T_(kd)(S). The hexagonal unbounded reference supplies such exact certificates for the identified moving cycles."
      ],
      [
        "Aperiodic motion is finite evidence",
        "A translated cluster on an aperiodic graph need not encounter the same surroundings forever. The Hat and Penrose travel records therefore state finite horizons. They do not prove an immortal Einstein glider. Exact stationary cycles do recur on their fixed finite graph; boundary separation is reported where measured."
      ],
      [
        "Launchers, emitters, guns and puffers",
        "A launcher produces outgoing bodies once. A recurring emitter regenerates a source over the tested interval. An indefinite gun claim needs a repeatable source and proof that outgoing bodies never interfere with later emissions. H03 is a finite aperiodic emitter. The identified hexagonal gun and puffers have separate separation certificates; a growing pufferâ€™s complete state is not a spaceship."
      ],
      [
        "Measurements",
        "N counts all nonzero cells; L counts state-1 cells. They differ for multistate rules. Cycle territory is the union of nonzero sites throughout a cycle. Boundary distance counts graph steps, with the outermost cells at zero. Polygon matches use the tolerances recorded in the evidence; they are not symbolic geometric proofs."
      ],
      [
        "Figures",
        "All 267 pattern panels are snapshots from the production engine and retain every nonzero cell. Pale cells are optional immediate zero-state context. Stationary cycle panels use one fixed view and scale; other panels are independently centred and scaled without rotation. The caption states the convention. Colours identify states. Every snapshot retains a complete-state hash and exact polygon coordinates in the source data."
      ]
    ],
    "neighbours": [
      [
        "Definition",
        "Shared-edge means a common boundary segment of positive length. Touch means edge or boundary-point contact, including a corner meeting another tileâ€™s edge at a T-junction. The examined cell is excluded. Hat and Penrose neighbour counts vary with position."
      ],
      [
        "Regular lattices",
        "A square touch neighbourhood has eight cells: four sharing edges and four diagonal corner contacts. A square edge neighbourhood has four. All ten curated square configurations use touch. The hexagonal edge and touch graphs coincide: at each vertex only three hexagons meet and every pair shares an edge. There are exactly six neighbours."
      ],
      [
        "Coordinates and borders",
        "Hexagonal axial neighbours differ by (1,0), (1,-1), (0,-1), (-1,0), (-1,1), or (0,1). A cell (q,r) has centre (sqrt(3)(q+r/2), 3r/2). Square coordinates are integer column/row coordinates. Hat/Penrose tile IDs depend on the released builder, depth and seed. A border parameter b locks cells at graph-boundary distance less than b to state 0. There is no wraparound; external missing cells are not additional state-0 neighbours in a priority table."
      ]
    ],
    "status": [
      [
        "Complete rule identifiers",
        "R01-R56 each fixes the tiling, neighbour relation, transition and C. The seed, patch size and locked border then specify an experiment. The same B/S digits on different graphs or with different C values describe different automata. R40:S5 uniquely identifies one survival clause; R15.1 identifies row 1 of the ordered R15 table."
      ],
      [
        "Standard, previously used, or original?",
        "Binary birth/survival, Generations refractory rules and ordered state-count tables are established formalisms. A rule card names its documented earlier author or user and the publication that contains it. If the package transfers standard counts to a different graph, the card names that standard rule and graph change. If no earlier use of a complete graph/update/state-count specification was identified, the card says so explicitly and dates that statement to 14 October 2026. An adapted table names its parent: R13 derives its mature mechanism from Edmondâ€™s Spectre relay [2,3]; R14 derives from his Hat B table R11 [2,3]. R15 was evolved in this package; Edmondâ€™s work is background for its search framework, not a source for that exact table."
      ],
      [
        "Rules and patterns have separate credit",
        "R10 and R11 are Edmondâ€™s published Hat rules. H02 and H03 are separately searched launch/source seeds under those rules and their known travelling mechanism. Calling B3/S23 Conway does not attribute a Hat, Penrose or hexagonal arrangement to Conway. The XOR construction uses a known additive principle. Each entry gives both its pattern status and a link to the ruleâ€™s status."
      ],
      [
        "Unresolved identity",
        "P09, penrose-conway-p12, matches the period 12, territory 33 and minimum population 6 of Owens and Stepneyâ€™s fireworks oscillator r-p12-33-6. These invariants do not determine identity. The exact geometric comparison is unresolved, and the pattern is not claimed original [5,6]."
      ]
    ],
    "reproduce": [
      [
        "From the application",
        "Use the version 3.7.4 release accompanying this catalogue. Open aperiodic-life-standalone.html, locate the entry by title in Curated configurations, and press Load. The lower-case identifier printed on the page is its discoveries.js key. Step through the generations shown. Save state preserves all labelled states. The live website may later run a different release."
      ],
      [
        "Machine-readable initial states",
        "The PDF attachment catalogue-data.json contains every preset, exact seed, rule and measurement. The repositoryâ€™s geometry/diagrams.json additionally records complete snapshot geometry, graph hashes and the realised initial states of random fields. Where a long seed is not printed in full, the record key, population and SHA-256 of the canonical seed list identify the attached exact data. PDF readers may hide attachments; the same data is provided as a normal file beside this PDF."
      ],
      [
        "Rebuilding the catalogue",
        "The editable prose is catalogue.md. catalogue-source.json contains exact definitions, seeds, attributions and bibliography. build_catalogue.py reads both and produces the PDF plus catalogue-data.json. Install the optional documentation dependencies in requirements.txt, then run python3 docs/catalogue/build_catalogue.py from the repository root. The application itself still needs no installation or npm dependencies."
      ],
      [
        "Regenerating scientific figures",
        "Run node docs/catalogue/export_geometry.js to regenerate every snapshot from core.js and discoveries.js. Then rebuild the PDF. Re-run node research/verify-catalog.js and the cited specialised verifiers when changing seeds, geometry or dynamics. A larger aperiodic depth changes tile IDs: transfer by verified polygon correspondence, not by assuming the same numerical ID means the same location."
      ]
    ],
    "integrity": [
      [
        "Double-checks",
        "All 89 curated presets are included exactly once, all 12 named UI rule presets are inventoried, and all 56 complete rule definitions are mapped to their entries. Every panel is regenerated from the release engine. The full catalogue verifier and the eleven published hexagonal source comparisons were rerun. Independent period/displacement and aperiodic structure checks from the previous catalogue remain applicable to the unchanged seeds and engine; the evidence records state their actual horizons."
      ],
      [
        "Reproducibility and scope",
        "The embedded catalogue data and repository source identify the exact engine and discovery file by SHA-256. The PDF deliberately does not embed its containing release archive. That would create a circular dependency. Build and verification output paths are described in the catalogue README. Source claims are qualified separately from the runtime checks."
      ],
      [
        "Corrections and citation",
        "Cite Aperiodic Life 3.7.4, the stable preset ID, the full rule including C, and the graph. A useful earlier-match report supplies a dated source, complete multistate seed, rule and neighbourhood, and an explicit transformation or phase match. Please submit corrections and patches to the project repository. Neither this software nor this computational catalogue establishes global priority or replaces independent verification."
      ]
    ],
