        "Stephen Silverâ€™s Life Lexicon, entry â€œgliderâ€ [26], explicitly credits Richard K. Guy. The package seed is a rotated and translated standard glider phase. No discovery year is asserted here."
      ]
    ],
    "pattern:square-lwss": [
      [
        "Verified behaviour",
        "The nine-cell LWSS translates two squares horizontally every four Conway generations."
      ],
      [
        "Interpretation and limits",
        "The infinite-lattice conclusion follows from the exact translated recurrence. The finite browser board is not infinite and eventually obstructs the flight."
      ],
      [
        "Pattern provenance and credit",
        "Stephen Silverâ€™s Life Lexicon, entry â€œLWSSâ€ [27], credits John H. Conwayâ€™s 1970 discovery of the lightweight spaceship. The package uses the published nine-cell phase, translated for display, under ordinary B3/S23."
      ]
    ],
    "pattern:square-highlife-replicator": [
      [
        "Verified behaviour",
        "At generation 12, the complete state is exactly the disjoint union of two copies of the 12-cell initial seed, translated by (-2,-2) and (2,2) in square coordinates. This is the established B36/S23 HighLife replicator."
      ],
      [
        "Interpretation and limits",
        "The direct check establishes the stated replication event. It does not by itself prove independent binary doubling forever: copies later interact, and the finite board limits growth."
      ],
      [
        "Pattern provenance and credit",
        "David Eppsteinâ€™s Replicators: HighLife [17] credits this replicator to Nathan Thompson in 1994. David Bell subsequently documented and investigated HighLife; he is not credited as this seedâ€™s discoverer. The package uses the published twelve-cell seed [28] and independently checks its two-copy return after twelve generations."
      ]
    ],
    "pattern:square-diehard": [
      [
        "Verified behaviour",
        "A famous seven-cell Conway methuselah that vanishes exactly at generation 130."
      ],
      [
        "Interpretation and limits",
        "This is an extinction transient, not an oscillator. The retained seed matches the classical published diehard; no individual discoverer is assigned without a source."
      ],
      [
        "Pattern provenance and credit",
        "The seven-cell seed matches diehard in Stephen Silverâ€™s Life Lexicon [29] exactly after translation. Its extinction at generation 130 is independently checked. The consulted source does not establish an individual discoverer; no personal attribution or new-discovery claim is made."
      ]
    ],
    "pattern:square-r-pentomino": [
      [
        "Verified behaviour",
        "The standard five-cell seed remains active through generation 1,103 on the supplied finite board. Independent square-lattice replay first differs from that board at generation 641; at generation 1,103 the infinite-lattice and finite-board populations are 116 and 111 respectively."
      ],
      [
        "Interpretation and limits",
        "The browser uses a finite 301 by 301 board with a permanently dead outer ring. The endpoint and population of that run must not be presented as the infinite-plane final census; escaping objects can encounter its boundary."
      ],
      [
        "Pattern provenance and credit",
        "Martin Gardnerâ€™s original Conway Life article [13] documents John H. Conwayâ€™s study of the R-pentomino; Stephen Silverâ€™s Life Lexicon [30] records the same five-cell shape. Its finite-board check establishes activity through generation 1,103, not equality with the infinite-plane final census."
      ]
    ],
    "pattern:square-acorn": [
      [
        "Verified behaviour",
        "The published seven-cell seed remains active through generation 5,206 on the supplied finite board. Independent square-lattice replay first differs from that board at generation 812; at generation 5,206 the infinite-lattice and finite-board populations are 633 and 620 respectively."
      ],
      [
        "Interpretation and limits",
        "The browser uses a finite 301 by 301 board with a permanently dead outer ring. Activity at generation 5,206 is a finite-board observation, not a certification of the infinite-plane ash or its stabilisation time."
      ],
      [
        "Pattern provenance and credit",
        "Robert Wainwrightâ€™s Lifeline No. 3 (1971), page 12 [31], credits Acorn to Charles L. Corderman; Wainwright supplied the name. Stephen Silverâ€™s Life Lexicon [32] records the same seven-cell seed. The package translates it for display and checks finite-board activity through 5,206 generations, not the infinite-plane final ash."
      ]
    ]
  },
